<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maly Trostenets</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maly Trostenets was a Nazi extermination site near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, in use from 1942 to 1944. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Minsk and others deported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, as well as Soviet prisoners and partisans, were shot there, mostly in the Blagovshchina forest, or killed in gas vans. Estimates of the number of dead range from about 60,000 to more than 200,000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maly-trostenets.jpg" alt="Maly Trostenets" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Epoz8 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maly_Trostinets_Field_of_Burial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
